## Story Generation and Evaluation (Reproducibility Checklist)

This section documents the story generation and evaluation setup used in the paper. The goal is to make the experimental pipeline transparent and directly replicable.

### A. Environment

- Hardware: MacBook Air (Apple M3)
- Runtime: Ollama (local inference)
- Python: REPLACE_ME (e.g., 3.10/3.11)
- Key libraries:
  - transformers (for Flan-T5 restoration; cite: Flan-T5/JMLR)
  - sentencepiece (cite: SentencePiece)
  - beautifulsoup4 (infobox parsing)
  - openai (for GPT-4o infobox linearization; cite: OpenAI model card / paper ref)
  - evaluation libs (cite: ROUGE, BLEU, BERTScore, chrF, BARTScore)

Checklist:
- [ ] Save environment metadata:
  - [ ] `ollama --version` → `outputs/env/ollama_version.txt`
  - [ ] `python --version` → `outputs/env/python_version.txt`
  - [ ] `pip freeze` / `conda env export` → `outputs/env/requirements.txt`
  - [ ] macOS version (`sw_vers`) → `outputs/env/macos_version.txt`

### B. Models (Pinned Artifacts)

We run story generation with two instruction-tuned open-weight LLMs:
- Baseline: Llama-3.1-Tulu-3-8B (cite: Tulu3)
- Comparator: Gemma-2-9b-it (cite: Gemma2)

Checklist:
- [ ] Record exact Ollama model identifiers:
  - [ ] `ollama list` → `outputs/env/ollama_list.txt`
  - [ ] `ollama show <MODEL_TAG>` → `outputs/env/<MODEL_TAG>_show.txt`
- [ ] Keep model tags fixed across runs (do not silently upgrade).

### C. Input Variants (Dataset Columns)

Story prompts are constructed from the following dataset fields:
- `history_section` (reference/target text for automatic evaluation)
- `history_excerpt_40` (partial context; first 40% of sentences)
- `info_box` (linearized infobox paragraph produced by GPT-4o)
- `infobox` (raw structured key–value metadata; used to produce `info_box`)

Input settings used in experiments:
- IB: `info_box`
- HE: `history_excerpt_40`
- HE+IB: concatenation of `history_excerpt_40` and `info_box`

Checklist:
- [ ] Ensure the dataset file name and schema are unchanged:
  - [ ] `anatolia_ancient_sites.csv` committed
  - [ ] Column names match exactly
  - [ ] Wikipedia retrieval date is documented (e.g., 2025-07-04)

### D. Prompting Conditions

We evaluate:
- Prompting: Zero-shot vs Few-shot (5-shot)
- Tones (exact strings used in prompts):
  - Academic: `plain academic`
  - Narrative: `engaging and simple narrative`
  - Descriptive: `visualizable descriptive style`
  - Museum-guide: `museum-guide style`

Few-shot exemplars:
- Fixed set of five exemplars: Ephesus, Priene, Termessos, Alacahoyuk, Gordion
- Exemplars are isolated from the test set used for reporting.

Checklist:
- [ ] Store all prompt templates under `prompts/`:
  - [ ] `prompts/prompt_template.txt` (system role + instructions)
  - [ ] `prompts/tone_modifiers.json` (tone → modifier string)
  - [ ] `prompts/fewshot_examples.json` (the 5 exemplars)
- [ ] Ensure few-shot examples are fixed (no random sampling at runtime).

### E. Generation Parameters (Ollama)

All story generations are executed locally via Ollama.

- We use Ollama’s default generation parameters as the baseline configuration.
- Prompt length varies by input setting and by few-shot vs zero-shot.

Checklist:
- [ ] Log generation configuration per run:
  - model tag
  - input mode (IB / HE / HE+IB)
  - tone
  - prompting type (zero-shot / 5-shot)
  - any overridden decoding options (if none, explicitly log "defaults")
- [ ] Save all raw generations (JSON/JSONL) under `outputs/generations/`

Note:
- If decoding uses sampling (non-zero temperature), repeated runs may yield minor variation.
  For exact replication of reported tables, use the provided `outputs/generations/` artifacts.

### F. Runtime / Latency Measurement (Local Feasibility)

We report wall-clock inference time measured on-device.
- Example baseline measurement (Tulu):
  - zero-shot: 42.1 s
  - 5-shot: 85.3 s

Checklist:
- [ ] Define timing method (e.g., `time.time()` around the call)
- [ ] Save raw timing logs → `outputs/timings/*.csv`

### G. Automatic Evaluation Metrics

We report:
- Baselines: ROUGE-L (cite: ROUGE), BLEU (cite: BLEU)
- Main metrics: BERTScore (F1) (cite: BERTScore), chrF (cite: chrF), BARTScore (cite: BARTScore)

Checklist:
- [ ] Save metric configs and versions:
  - tokenization settings / smoothing (if applicable)
  - BERTScore model used (e.g., RoBERTa-Large, etc.) if configured
  - BARTScore checkpoint and scoring settings
- [ ] Save per-sample metric outputs → `outputs/metrics/per_sample.csv`
- [ ] Save aggregated tables (mean over sites) → `outputs/metrics/summary_tables.csv`

### H. QA-Based Factuality Evaluation and Statistical Tests

We complement surface-form metrics with a QA-based factuality pipeline:
- For each evaluated sample, generate K = 3 questions from the reference text.
- Verify whether model answers are supported by the reference ("supported/unsupported").
- Sample-level factuality is the mean of K binary judgments (range [0,1]).

Checklist:
- [ ] Save full QA traces per sample:
  - questions, story answers, reference answers, decisions
  → `outputs/factuality/factuality_traces.jsonl`
- [ ] Save factuality scores per sample → `outputs/factuality/factuality_scores.csv`

Statistical testing:
- We use paired comparisons when the same sites are evaluated under two conditions.
- Report p-values from paired tests (e.g., paired t-test) and optionally a non-parametric robustness check.
- Do not hardcode p-values in code; compute them from the saved score files.

Checklist:
- [ ] Save statistical test outputs → `outputs/stats/factuality_tests.csv`
- [ ] Record the exact pairing rule used (e.g., same city; same prompt_type).


### I. Minimal “Run Order” Summary

1. Dataset is already provided: `anatolia_ancient_sites.csv`
2. (Optional) Re-run preprocessing:
   - history restoration (SentencePiece + Flan-T5; cite: SentencePiece, Flan-T5, CoEdIT)
   - infobox parsing + GPT-4o linearization (cite: GPT-4o/OpenAI)
3. Generate stories via Ollama for all combinations:
   - input modes: IB / HE / HE+IB
   - tones: Academic / Narrative / Descriptive / Museum-guide
   - prompting: zero-shot / 5-shot
4. Compute automatic metrics and export summary tables.
5. Run QA-based factuality evaluation and paired statistical tests.

